diagnosis_text:
I reproduced the reported path end-to-end and found the fabric healthy and host reachability working now. From pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 (and back), pings succeed 0% loss with ~1.3 ms RTT, so the basic host-to-host path is up. On the routed underlay/overlay, every inter-switch BGP session is Established with prefixes exchanged: both leafs (leaf_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1) show two spine neighbors up; both spines (spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1) have three neighbors up (super-spine plus both leafs); and the super-spine has both spine neighbors up. Each neighbor shows received prefixes (not Idle/Active), indicating no switch-to-switch link is down.

Mechanistically, a failed switch-to-switch cable would drop the eBGP session bound to that link, reduce PfxRcd to 0, and force reroute or blackhole traffic; we see none of that. Localization across all routing nodes confirms intact adjacencies and consistent reachability; no single device or interface exhibits fault symptoms.

Root cause: No network fault present at this time; the earlier outage was either transient or endpoint-related (outside the fabric).

Corrective fix: No fabric changes required. Please re-test from the application/host side. If the issue recurs, capture timestamps and run a quick ping from each host; also check host firewalls/NIC state. We can enable link/BGP flap logging to catch any intermittent physical instability, but today all spine/leaf/super-spine links and sessions are healthy.
=== END ===